{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs may avoid arrest if they agree to enter a behavioral health initiative and engage in a diversion pathway designed to steer them away from incarceration. However, failure to meet program conditions could lead to up to half a year behind bars.\n\n***\n\nThe updated framework empowers law enforcement to respond more decisively to public drug use and imposes stricter consequences for distribution near sensitive areas such as playgrounds. While counties are encouraged to establish health-focused alternatives, participation is not required, leading to concerns about inconsistent implementation across regions. Police are instructed to assess each case quickly: if someone qualifies for the alternative route, they must be connected with a mobile health team within a half-hour window—or face detention.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief emphasized the narrow conditions for avoiding charges: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no acute medical emergencies. The system relies on rapid coordination between officers and health responders. If the team can’t arrive in time, detention becomes unavoidable. Critics argue the process lacks flexibility and may disproportionately impact vulnerable populations who don’t meet the strict criteria.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to handle substance use. In some parts of the country, people are still being jailed for possession, while others explore more humane approaches. Imagine a teenager who just turned 18, trying to eat a sandwich after a long day, while their parent is shopping for groceries—and wondering why their tongue feels numb from stress. The system must do better.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.1409779787063599}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.14854338765144348}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.00033122775494121015}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.2234618663787842}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.04941719025373459}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -6.69933797325939e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.002995648654177785}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"toke